    id: "net-problem-details", category: ".NET", title: "Exception handling and Problem Details",
    summary: "Separate expected business outcomes from unexpected faults and return safe errors.",
    what: "An exception reports an exceptional control-flow outcome, while a business rejection can be an ordinary result. Problem Details provides a structured HTTP error representation. A consistent error boundary maps application outcomes without exposing internal implementation details.",
    why: "A 200 response containing an error string misleads clients and monitoring. Conversely, treating every unavailable order as a server failure inflates incident signals and encourages inappropriate retries.",
    how: "Define expected outcomes first: invalid input, missing data, forbidden actions, and concurrency conflicts. Map them deliberately to the public contract. Place centralized exception handling around endpoint execution, log unexpected faults with correlation information, and return a generic server-error detail. Avoid logging sensitive payloads or treating a caller-disconnected cancellation as an ordinary application crash. Once a response has started, replacing its body may no longer be possible.",
