    id: "net-jwt-policies", category: ".NET", title: "JWT authentication and resource authorization",
    summary: "Validate token trust, evaluate policies, and enforce ownership at the resource boundary.",
    what: "Bearer authentication establishes a principal from a valid credential. A JWT is a token format, not proof of trust merely because its payload can be decoded. Authorization then decides whether that principal may perform an operation on a particular resource.",
    why: "A correctly signed token for another API or an unrelated tenant must not grant access. Role checks alone cannot prove that the requested order belongs to the caller.",
    how: "Use a maintained authentication handler and configure trusted issuer, audience, signature validation, and lifetime checks. Require HTTPS and avoid logging bearer credentials. Apply coarse policies at endpoints, then evaluate resource ownership using trusted identity and server-side data. Distinguish an invalid or absent identity from an authenticated but forbidden principal. Cookie-based browser flows additionally need their own CSRF defenses; CORS does not replace authorization.",
    flow: "Bearer token -> cryptographic and claim validation -> principal -> policy -> resource ownership -> operation",
    example: "// After configuring JWT bearer authentication with the trusted provider:\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>\n options.AddPolicy(\"OrdersRead\", policy =>\n  policy.RequireAuthenticatedUser().RequireClaim(\"permission\", \"orders.read\")));\n// Endpoint policy plus an ownership check is still required.",
    cheat: ["Decoding is not validation.", "Authentication and authorization solve different problems.", "401 and 403 are not interchangeable.", "An identity token is not automatically an API access token."],
