    id: "net-httpclient", category: ".NET", title: "HttpClient, connection reuse, and deadlines",
    summary: "Make outbound calls without exhausting sockets or hiding uncertain write outcomes.",
    what: "HttpClient sends HTTP requests using underlying connection handlers. Factory-managed clients or deliberately managed long-lived clients allow reuse without creating a new connection pool for every operation. Timeouts and cancellation bound waiting; they do not prove that the remote operation did not happen.",
    why: "Frequent client and handler creation wastes connections, while unsuitable connection lifetimes can delay adaptation to address changes. Unbounded downstream waits retain request work and amplify outages.",
    how: "Configure a named or typed client with a trusted base address, suitable handler lifetime behavior, and a bounded timeout. Pass cancellation through reads and dispose responses. Apply retries only where transient failure and operation semantics justify them, inside one total deadline. Do not retry a create-payment request merely because its response was lost; use the provider's idempotency contract and outcome lookup. Review cookies and handler scopes before using shared handlers for user-specific state.",
