    id: "net-workers-channels", category: ".NET", title: "BackgroundService and bounded Channels",
    summary: "Move appropriate work off the HTTP path with explicit capacity and shutdown behavior.",
    what: "A hosted background service participates in the application's lifetime. Channels coordinate producers and consumers within a process. A bounded channel limits pending work, but it does not make queued data durable across a restart.",
    why: "Fire-and-forget request tasks can lose failures, use disposed request dependencies, or disappear when the process exits. An unlimited queue turns slow processing into uncontrolled memory growth.",
    how: "Give the worker a cancellation-aware processing loop and create a dependency scope for each appropriate work unit. Choose queue capacity and full behavior from the workload, not convenience. Define whether shutdown drains, cancels, or checkpoints work within the host's deadline. Persist important jobs before acknowledging acceptance, using a durable queue or job store when restarts must not lose them. Observe worker exceptions and expose queue age as well as depth.",
