    id: "net-performance", category: ".NET", title: "Performance profiling and thread-pool starvation",
    summary: "Diagnose CPU, allocation, blocking, and database bottlenecks before optimizing.",
    what: "Application performance is the result of work, waiting, contention, and resource limits. Thread-pool starvation occurs when useful work waits because workers are occupied or unavailable, often aggravated by blocking. High CPU, growing memory, and slow SQL are different diagnostic paths.",
    why: "Adding replicas or wrapping blocking code in Task.Run can move pressure without fixing it. An optimization that reduces one microbenchmark's allocations may not improve the user-facing critical path.",
    how: "Reproduce a representative load with warm-up and explicit concurrency. Observe CPU, allocation, GC behavior, worker queues, request latency, and database timing. Use runtime counters, traces, or dumps according to the symptom and protect captured diagnostic data. Find the dominant cost, change one relevant mechanism, and compare the same workload and error rate. Preserve resource bounds while optimizing.",
