    summary: "Separate update rules from query shapes without assuming separate databases are mandatory.",
    what: "CQRS separates command responsibilities from query responsibilities. A command expresses an intended state change; a query returns a view without that change. The separation can begin in one application and one store, with independent projections introduced only when justified.",
    why: "Complex writes need invariant protection, while screens often need denormalized joins and summaries. Forcing both through the same object graph can make queries expensive and updates difficult to validate.",
